# beckon bindings for i3. Save at ~/.config/i3/beckon.config and add
# this line to your ~/.config/i3/config:
#
#   include ~/.config/i3/beckon.config
#
# Reload: i3-msg reload (or $mod+Shift+r if you keep the default).
#
# i3 and sway share the same IPC protocol, so beckon uses the same
# backend. The only difference: i3 sets `I3SOCK` instead of `SWAYSOCK`.
#
# Discover ids on your machine:
#   beckon installed          list installed apps
#   beckon list               list currently running apps
#   beckon search files       fuzzy search
#   beckon resolve Spotify    validate an id

set $beckon exec beckon

# Ctrl + Super + Alt, the same chord every other example here uses, in the order
# the three keys sit in on the bottom row. `Mod4` is Super and `Mod1` is Alt —
# X11's own names, and the spelling the sway file uses too.
#
# It is NOT `$mod`: that is your own variable with your own bindings on it, and
# a sourced file has no business taking five of your letters.
set $beckonMod Ctrl+Mod4+Mod1

# `t` for the terminal, matching every other example here. This file used to
# bind it to Space, which is the one binding that contradicted the rest.
bindsym $beckonMod+t     $beckon kitty
bindsym $beckonMod+c     $beckon "Google Chrome"
bindsym $beckonMod+v     $beckon "Visual Studio Code"
bindsym $beckonMod+f     $beckon Files
bindsym $beckonMod+s     $beckon Spotify
